{"causes":["The Roman Empire's decline and the rise of Christianity in the 1st century CE created an intellectual vacuum that the rabbis sought to fill by developing and codifying Jewish law and tradition.","The destruction of the Second Temple in 70 CE led to a renewed focus on textual study and interpretation, contributing to the growth of oral traditions that would eventually be compiled into the Talmud.","The Babylonian exile (586-539 BCE) fostered a diasporic community of Jews who preserved and transmitted Jewish texts through memory and written records."],"effects":["The compilation of the Talmud helped solidify Jewish law and custom, providing a framework for understanding the relationships between God, Israel, and the world.","The influence of the Talmud can be seen in Western philosophical debates about morality, justice, and the nature of truth, shaping thinkers such as Maimonides and Spinoza.","The Talmud's literary legacy has inspired countless works of Jewish literature and continues to resonate with readers around the world."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"rabbinic-judaism","sourceName":"Rabbinic Judaism","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"talmud","targetName":"The Talmud","context":"A central text within this tradition."},{"sourceSlug":"hebrew-bible","sourceName":"Hebrew Bible","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"talmud","targetName":"The Talmud","context":"Providing commentary and interpretation."},{"sourceSlug":"philosophy-western","sourceName":"Western Philosophy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"talmud","targetName":"The Talmud","context":"Through its emphasis on rational inquiry and debate."},{"sourceSlug":"roman-empire","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"talmud","targetName":"The Talmud","context":"The destruction of the Second Temple."},{"sourceSlug":"christianity","sourceName":"Christianity","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"talmud","targetName":"The Talmud","context":"Creating a need for alternative interpretations and traditions."},{"sourceSlug":"babylonian-exile","sourceName":"Babylonian Exile","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"talmud","targetName":"The Talmud","context":"Preserving and transmitting Jewish texts through memory and written records."},{"sourceSlug":"jewish-law","sourceName":"Jewish Law","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"talmud","targetName":"The Talmud","context":"Providing a framework for understanding Jewish custom and practice."},{"sourceSlug":"western-philosophy","sourceName":"Western Philosophy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"talmud","targetName":"The Talmud","context":"In debates about morality, justice, and the nature of truth."}],"places":[{"name":"Jerusalem","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Babylon","role":"Major center of study and compilation"},{"name":"Rome","role":"Capital city that witnessed significant events in Jewish-Christian relations"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Talmud","type":"Reference article","slug":"talmud"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q43290","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talmud"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Talmud left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 125 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Talmud (Hebrew: תַּלְמוּד, 'Teaching' or 'Study') is the central text of Rabbinic Judaism — a vast compilation of Jewish oral law (halakha), legal discussion (mahloket), biblical commentary (aggadah), ethical teachings, folklore, and wisdom literature, organised as a commentary on the Mishnah (the earlier codification of Jewish oral law, redacted c. 200 CE by Rabbi Judah ha-Nasi). There are two Talmuds: the Jerusalem Talmud (Talmud Yerushalmi, compiled in Palestine c. 350–400 CE) and the Babylonian Talmud (Talmud Bavli, compiled in Babylonia c. 500–600 CE) — the Babylonian Talmud is the authoritative text of Rabbinic Judaism, studied and interpreted continuously from the 6th century to the present day. The Babylonian Talmud comprises approximately 2.7 million words across 63 tractates (masekhtot) organised into six orders (sedarim): Zeraim (agriculture), Moed (festivals), Nashim (women), Nezikin (damages), Kodashim (holy things), and Tohorot (purity).

The Talmud developed from the oral tradition of interpretation of the Hebrew Bible (Torah) — the Pharisaic tradition of 'oral Torah' that the rabbis claimed was given to Moses at Sinai alongside the written Torah. Its characteristic mode is dialectical debate: the Talmud records the discussions of rabbinical academies across multiple generations, preserving minority opinions alongside majority decisions, recording the arguments by which legal conclusions are reached rather than simply stating the conclusions, and creating a multi-layered text in which different generations of rabbis debate across centuries. The names of hundreds of rabbis — Hillel, Shammai, Akiva, Meir, Yochanan, Rava, Abaye — are recorded as they argue about legal, ethical, and theological questions, creating a unique form of canonical literature that functions as an encyclopaedia of debate.

The Talmud is not only the central legal text of Rabbinic Judaism but the primary intellectual formation of Jewish culture for over fifteen centuries — the yeshiva (rabbinical academy) tradition of Talmud study, the practice of havruta (paired textual study), and the Talmudic style of dialectical reasoning have shaped Jewish intellectual culture, and through it, Jewish contributions to European and global intellectual life.","subjects":["Jewish Literature","Ancient Era","Rabbinic Judaism","Jewish Law","Halakha","Oral Tradition","Religious Texts","Intellectual History"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Talmud has played a pivotal role in shaping Jewish thought and practice, influencing Western philosophy and ethics more broadly, while remaining a subject of ongoing scholarly debate and interpretation.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
